ibility: unknown. Adaptability: not willing to further education (negative). Leadership? Not mentioned.\n\nD: Reliability: takes responsibility seriously (positive). Stress resilience: responds adequately to unexpected events (some). Technical competence: solves problems extremely well, concentration (positive). Decision quality: solves problems extremely well (positive). Crew cooperation: know-it-all, hot temper (negative). Professional communication: unknown. Adaptability: unknown. Leadership: weak.\n\nWe need to pick best overall. B has strong reliability and stress, but crew cooperation issues. C has strong across many but crew cooperation and adaptability issues. A has strong decision quality and leadership but unknown stress and attention, plus organization issues. D has strong technical and responsibility but crew cooperation issues.\n\nWhich candidate best balances? The panel seems to think B's issues can be mitigated via training, while A's unknown stress is a bigger risk. C's egocentric and unwillingness to learn may be hard to mitigate. D's hot temper also problematic.\n\nThus likely B remains best. As Agent 1, we can either maintain A or shift to B. Given the evidence, we might shift to B, acknowledging that A's unknown stress is a critical gap, and B's known stress handling outweighs his cooperation issues which can be mitigated.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: Agent 2 and 3 argued for B, Agent 1 argued for A. Now we can say after reviewing C and D, I still think A's unknown stress is a risk, and B's known stress handling and conscientiousness outweigh his cooperation concerns, which are specific and trainable. So I change my vote to Candidate B.\n\nWe must include explicit facts: B handles stress very well, very conscientious; A unorganized, not good at criticism; unknown stress for A; B can be uncooperative and wrong tone. Also note C's egocentric and not willing to further education, D's hot temper.\n\nThus final message: I shift vote to B,